{"type":"text","timestamp":1794358598973,"sessionID":"ses_e37e4f349ffe51J6s9LdQyAxPv","part":{"id":"prt_1c81b5d17001T2JjsBfavbO0Re","messageID":"msg_1c81b4b66001SF6esjFQdDVEOl","sessionID":"ses_e37e4f349ffe51J6s9LdQyAxPv","type":"text","text":"<think>Let me check the requirements.lock to see what's available, and check the sandbox network access for npm/pip.\n\nLet me plan the implementation:\n\nBackend:\n- `backend/app.py` — Flask app with:\n  - `/api/health`\n  - `/api/auth/register`, `/api/auth/login`\n  - `/api/base64/encode`, `/api/base64/decode`\n  - `/api/conversions` GET/POST, `/api/conversions/<id>` DELETE\n  - Serve frontend dist\n- SQLite in `./data`\n- Token-based auth: simple token stored in DB or signed token. Let's use itsdangerous or just a random token stored in a table `tokens`. Simplest: store tokens in the DB table `auth_tokens`, or use HMAC-signed tokens with itsdangerous (Flask dependency includes itsdangerous). I'll use itsdangerous with a secret key persisted in data dir, so tokens survive restart. Actually, storing token in DB is simple and reliable. But using itsdangerous is cleaner. Let me check itsdangerous is available in requirements.lock.\n\nPassword hashing: `werkzeug.security.generate_password_hash` — Flask depends on werkzeug. Good.\n\nFrontend:\n- React + TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4 (via @tailwindcss/vite) or v3 (postcss). Tailwind v4 is simpler with Vite plugin. Need to check npm availability offline. Let me check the npm cache.\n\nLet me check the requirements.lock contents and npm registryaccess.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794358598935,"end":1794358598968}}}
