{"type":"text","timestamp":1794359226562,"sessionID":"ses_e37e4f349ffe51J6s9LdQyAxPv","part":{"id":"prt_1c824f0ae001YUPrFLyDk5NjCH","messageID":"msg_1c824cca2001ucxLISsqmS2Miv","sessionID":"ses_e37e4f349ffe51J6s9LdQyAxPv","type":"text","text":"<think>No browser download possible (the CDN is blocked). So I can't run a real browser test. I'll have to verify the frontend by reasoning and by checking the built output, plus maybe a lightweight DOM test with jsdom + React Testing Library? That's heavy.\n\nAlternative: I can at least verify the frontend serves correctly and the API integration works via curl. And I can do a static review of the React code for correctness.\n\nActually, I could use `jsdom` via npm (available in the registry) to render the App and simulate interactions — that would catch runtime errors. Let me consider: install jsdom + @testing-library/react + vitest as devDependencies? That would add dependencies to the project that aren't needed for the app (and the Dockerfile installs devDeps during the build → slower but fine). Hmm, but it pollutes the repo. I could install them in a separate temp directory outside the project... but npm install in /tmp was blocked earlier? The block was on the venv creation in /tmp/opencode (external directory rule). Hmm, the earlier denial was for `python3 -m venv venv` in /tmp/opencode/work. Interesting — the mkdir -p worked? Let me test if I can write to /tmp/opencode/work.\n\nActually, the deny rule was \"external_directory\" with pattern \"*\" and action \"ask\" → the tool asked and I got denied for that specific call. Let me avoid external dirs and instead create a temporary test setup inside /workspace (e.g., /workspace/.tmp-test) and delete it afterward.\n\nLet me evaluate: is a jsdom-based test worth it? It would verify:\n- The app renders without crashing.\n- The encode/decode flow calls the API and shows results.\n- Register/sign-in flow.\n- History rendering.\n\nThat's valuable since I can't use a browser. Let me do it with vitest + jsdom + @testing-library/react. I'll install these as devDependencies temporarily in a separate folder to avoid polluting package.json... but then the imports of the app source need to resolve. I could put the test files in /workspace/frontend/src/__tests__ and install the deps temporarily, then remove them from package.json + lock at the end? The lock file churn is a hassle.\n\nAlternative: keep the test deps in devDependencies permanently? The README says dependencies are pinned and the Dockerfile installs them → the Docker build would install vitest etc. (slower, larger image). Not ideal but acceptable? I'd rather keep the shipped deps lean.\n\nPlan: temporarily install vitest/jsdom/@testing-library/react into frontend/ (adding to package.json + lock), write the tests, run them, then revert package.json/package-lock.json (git checkout) and delete the test files. The node_modules dir stays but is gitignored (check .gitignore — hmm, is there a .gitignore? Let me check. The repo has no .gitignore at the root? .dockerignore exists but .gitignore wasn't in the listing. Let me check git status to see if node_modules would be committed... The repo listing showed no .gitignore. I should add one for node_modules/.venv/data/dist to keep the repo clean.\n\nLet me check git status now.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794359226542,"end":1794359226558}}}
